# Code owners.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES TODAY: it requests a review. Nothing more.
#
# It is not a merge gate here, and describing it as one would be worse than
# having no file at all — a control that is believed to exist is not audited.
# The repository's ruleset, verified read-only rather than assumed:
#
#   required_approving_review_count = 0
#   require_code_owner_review       = false
#
# Both are 0/false because the repository has one human account. GitHub does
# not count a pull request author's own approval, so requiring one review would
# make every change unmergeable by the only person who can make it. Turning
# these on needs a second GitHub account or an organization team with at least
# one other member; until that exists, this file marks the paths worth looking
# at, and looking at them is a habit rather than an enforced step.
#
# The paths below are still the right ones — that judgement does not depend on
# whether a rule enforces it.
#
# Owner resolved from the repository's GitHub metadata, not assumed:
#   gh api repos/aydgnme/Potriv --jq .owner.login  ->  aydgnme

# Everything, as the fallback. Later matches win, so the specific entries
# below still apply where they overlap.
*                                                   @aydgnme

# ---- CI and release automation -------------------------------------------
# A workflow change can disable a required check or exfiltrate a secret, so it
# is reviewed even when the rest of the change looks routine.
/.github/                                           @aydgnme
/.github/workflows/                                 @aydgnme

